nping for talks in Beijing in October, the Kremlin said on Tuesday, Putin\u2019s first known trip abroad since an arrest warrant was issued against him over the deportation of children from Ukraine. Nikolai Patrushev, a close Putin ally and the secretary of Russia\u2019s Security Council, said Russia and China should deepen cooperation in the face of the West\u2019s attempt to contain them both. He was quoted by the Interfax news agency as saying that the talks in Beijing would be \u201cthorough\u201d, at a meeting in Moscow with China\u2019s top diplomat, Wang Yi. Putin will attend the third Belt and Road Forum after an invitation by Xi during a high-profile visit to Moscow in March. Days before that visit, the International Criminal Court (ICC) issued a warrant for Putin\u2019s arrest on suspicion of illegally deporting hundreds of children or more from Ukraine. Moscow denies the allegations and the Kremlin said the warrant was evidence of the West\u2019s hostility to Russia, which opened a criminal case against the ICC prosecutor and the judges who issued the warrant. Russia\u2019s full-scale invasion of Ukraine early last year has triggered one of the deadliest European conflicts since World War Two and the biggest confrontation between Moscow and the West since the 1962 Cuban Missile Crisis. Putin has pivoted towards China, and Xi has stood by him. Chinese-Russian trade has soared since the invasion, and Russia has sold Asian powers including China greater volumes of the oil it can no longer sell to the West because of sanctions. Putin last visited Beijing in February 2022, days before the invasion, where he and Xi announced a \u2018no limits\u2019 partnership. Moscow says this does not mean a military alliance, however. The United States sees China as its gravest long-term \u201cstrategic competitor\u201d and Russia as an \u201cacute threat\u201d. China has refrained from condemning Russia\u2019s operation against Ukraine or calling it an invasion, in line with the Kremlin, which casts the war as a \u201cspecial military operation\u201d. Earlier this year, CIA Director William Burns said Russia was becoming more and more dependent on China and was even at risk of becoming its \u201ceconomic colony\u201d in time.", "language": "en", "metadata": {"sentence_id": "1"}, "frames": [{"name": "Documents", "roles": [{"name": "Bearer", "filler": "arrest", "idxs": [51, 51]}], "idxs": [52, 52], "activation": "warrant"}, {"name": "Travel", "roles": [{"name": "Traveler", "filler": "Putin \u2019s", "idxs": [43, 44]}, {"name": "Place", "filler": "abroad", "idxs": [48, 48]}, {"name": "Time", "filler": "since an arrest warrant was issued against him over the deportation of children from Ukraine", "idxs": [49, 63]}], "idxs": [47, 47], "activation": "trip"}, {"name": "Statement", "roles": [{"name": "Message", "filler": "on Tuesday , Putin \u2019s first known trip abroad since an arrest warrant was issued against him over the deportation of children from Ukraine", "idxs": [40, 63]}, {"name": "Message", "filler": "After North Korea \u2019s Kim Jong Un , Putin and Xi to meet in Beijing in October", "idxs": [0, 16]}, {"name": "Message", "filler": "Kremlin Russian President Vladimir Putin will meet China \u2019s Xi Jinping for talks in Beijing in October", "idxs": [19, 35]}, {"name": "Speaker", "filler": "the Kremlin", "idxs": [37, 38]}], "idxs": [39, 39], "activation": "said"}, {"name": "Time_vector", "roles": [{"name": "Landmark_event", "filler": "an arrest warrant was issued against him over the deportation of children from Ukraine", "idxs": [50, 63]}, {"name": "Event", "filler": "Putin \u2019s first known trip abroad", "idxs": [43, 48]}, {"name": "Direction", "filler": "since", "idxs": [49, 49]}], "idxs": [49, 49], "activation": "since"}, {"name": "Leadership", "roles": [{"name": "Leader", "filler": "Vladimir Putin", "idxs": [22, 23]}, {"name": "Governed", "filler": "Russian", "idxs": [20, 20]}], "idxs": [21, 21], "activation": "President"},